  "question": {
    "text": "A particle is executing linear simple harmonic motion of amplitude A. At what displacement is the energy of the particle half potential and half kinetic?",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "hlOww",
      "text": "$\\frac{\\mathrm{A}}{4}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "7BYBv",
      "text": "$\\frac{\\mathrm{A}}{2}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "WO5SL",
      "text": "$\\frac{\\mathrm{A}}{\\sqrt{2}}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "GowZw",
      "text": "$\\frac{\\mathrm{A}}{\\sqrt{3}}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Hints : Total Energy $(\\mathrm{E})=\\frac{1}{2} m \\omega^2 \\mathrm{~A}^2$\nP.E. $=\\frac{1}{2} m \\omega^2 x^2$\nAs P.E. $=\\frac{E}{2}$\nThen, $\\frac{1}{2} m \\omega^2 \\mathrm{~A}^2 \\times \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{1}{2} m \\omega^2 x^2$\n$$\nx^2=\\frac{\\mathrm{A}^2}{2} \\Rightarrow x=\\frac{\\mathrm{A}}{\\sqrt{2}}\n$$",
    "image": null
  },
